#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7e65b88-61b3-5d93-82e5-349c95167b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062587/assets/69e3e6eabe9e2ea5d28a6ff0feb40437965649af45ad247b572a35ac3bf642a4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343741} {:answer/id #uuid "98241003-60bb-5256-b8be-25f52dba3b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062587/assets/f9040d78661e0d33290ad14e1ddf815211985f5963c8e1c8b9b5438dd8f3931c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343742} {:answer/id #uuid "66332007-7e3f-53db-8605-325253d0b6c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062587/assets/d271c5f4cf0f250dcfdbba8addfdc5f8c583832f378756fc3066e2b961a31dc3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343743} {:answer/id #uuid "696e8632-6643-5ece-8ded-773adbd5981c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062587/assets/9c045d6467a91676cd27a4c11cbb651a798b2480662d09b446b52d8eeb490ff4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343744} {:answer/id #uuid "a774dc82-cfa2-59b4-b4d9-952453262bc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062587/assets/d38c8f370e25add2a06ecc652127bae817e2c8bc394f4db6fdf539fbfef3750c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "696e8632-6643-5ece-8ded-773adbd5981c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062587/assets/9c045d6467a91676cd27a4c11cbb651a798b2480662d09b446b52d8eeb490ff4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343744} :answer-field/id #uuid "e846320e-efc4-5779-b956-87c1c7267b26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "295bcdf6-b38c-5643-831e-9c87f25a6f53" :question/math-academy-id "q-147611" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062587/assets/27c1ad7d429eb8da46b0bb3baec048b3564ce206b4523416cb4cb56b0a33767f.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(3,-6)$, $(0,0),$ $(6,-12).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062587/assets/9c045d6467a91676cd27a4c11cbb651a798b2480662d09b446b52d8eeb490ff4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee120c51-ad83-5457-8bd8-a6fcbc510d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102212} {:answer/id #uuid "010f9ebd-8bc1-5fb4-8cf9-7872bec9db2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102213} {:answer/id #uuid "055b1bbe-5e9c-58a6-8fe1-f26400043b55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102214} {:answer/id #uuid "0967e215-a9e0-5603-bd67-e07fb561833b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102215} {:answer/id #uuid "53a202b2-0c63-5178-876a-bb66ea00d715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee120c51-ad83-5457-8bd8-a6fcbc510d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A quadrilateral" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102212} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8285fd1-5cf3-5d3c-acc9-9aaef2c12a2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f03e3e5-8f9e-5206-8323-aab2c0298778" :question/math-academy-id "q-162206" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062587/assets/e6d0ce147aec90c3840a893e36596fbadf981ff96559f65d055f4486406cca7f.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the quadrilateral shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a nonsingular matrix. Therefore, the image of our object under the action of  $T$ is a quadrilateral."}]}
